WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:04.003
- On this episode
Krewella stops by.

00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:07.003
(hip hop music)

00:00:15.117 --> 00:00:17.816
- [Gary] You ask questions,

00:00:17.816 --> 00:00:20.903
and I answer them.

00:00:20.903 --> 00:00:25.718
This is The #AskGaryVee Show.

00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:31.944
- Hey everybody, 
this is Gary Vay-ner-chuk

00:00:31.944 --> 00:00:36.398
and this is episode 215 
of The #AskGaryVee Show.

00:00:36.398 --> 00:00:39.460
And this is a fun show I got a
whole crew with me and I'm in

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:42.042
the VaynerMedia LA office as we

00:00:42.042 --> 00:00:44.311
normally drill this 
in New York City.

00:00:44.311 --> 00:00:45.779
I'm thrilled to be here.

00:00:45.779 --> 00:00:49.249
I'm excited about this episode 
I think we actually have similar

00:00:49.249 --> 00:00:52.419
backgrounds in to how we got to
this moment so actually

00:00:52.419 --> 00:00:53.553
let's start with that.

00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:56.957
Vayner Nation, why don't you
guys introduce yourself to

00:00:56.957 --> 00:00:58.592
everybody. Why don't you 
tell them who you

00:00:58.592 --> 00:01:00.460
are and what you do?

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:01.895
- I'm Jahan.
- I'm Yasmine.

00:01:01.895 --> 00:01:03.196
- We are Krewella.

00:01:03.196 --> 00:01:05.683
- This is our manager,
Jake, one of our managers.

00:01:05.683 --> 00:01:06.767
- Hi.

00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:09.369
- For everybody who's
watching, what is Krewella?

00:01:09.369 --> 00:01:11.271
What do you guys do?

00:01:11.271 --> 00:01:12.072
Where'd you guys come from?

00:01:12.072 --> 00:01:14.474
What kind of music genre,
like, how do you guys roll?

00:01:14.474 --> 00:01:15.642
- We're from Chicago.

00:01:15.642 --> 00:01:17.311
Gotta get that in first.

00:01:17.311 --> 00:01:18.845
- I graduated
with Jake, this guy.

00:01:18.845 --> 00:01:20.414
- Is that true?
- Yes.

00:01:20.414 --> 00:01:22.282
- We're all from Chicago.
- Hold on. Hold on.

00:01:22.282 --> 00:01:24.384
I did a little bit
of homework beforehand.

00:01:24.384 --> 00:01:26.386
Did you grow up, 
are you from Chicago or

00:01:26.386 --> 00:01:28.055
from a suburb of Chicago?

00:01:28.055 --> 00:01:30.822
- We're from a suburb of 
Chicago but we like rep

00:01:30.822 --> 00:01:33.627
the city because we 
definitely did live there.

00:01:33.627 --> 00:01:36.430
- On a side note this is
amazingly weird timing,

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:38.425
I'm obsessed right now
with Chicago hiphop.

00:01:38.425 --> 00:01:40.033
- Good.
- Oh great.

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:41.601
- Yes,--
- Like Nissa?

00:01:41.601 --> 00:01:44.592
- Yes but more so Chance The
Rapper and even I'm obsessed

00:01:44.592 --> 00:01:48.608
with Saba. I think Chicago is,
which by the way, this is a

00:01:48.608 --> 00:01:52.212
weird thing to say I don't get
beat up right now on my show

00:01:52.212 --> 00:01:54.815
I'm historically pretty
disrespectful to Chicago

00:01:54.815 --> 00:01:55.682
just as an FYI.

00:01:55.682 --> 00:01:57.317
I want to be authentic.
- Why is that?

00:01:58.518 --> 00:02:01.254
- Maybe because it's a
little New York bias and

00:02:01.254 --> 00:02:03.890
I'm disrespectful it every
city besides New York.

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:04.558
I don't know.

00:02:04.558 --> 00:02:07.427
I'm weird about Chicago it
makes me sad to say that because

00:02:07.427 --> 00:02:09.895
Brandon Warnke who runs Wine
Library, my best friend in the

00:02:09.895 --> 00:02:14.001
world, is a huge Cubs fan so 
I have a lot of love for that.

00:02:14.001 --> 00:02:15.535
I don't know.
It's always windy, I don't know.

00:02:15.535 --> 00:02:18.181
- But it gives you thick skin.

00:02:18.181 --> 00:02:21.208
If you're walking to work
everyday, walking to school and

00:02:21.208 --> 00:02:23.877
you have this
brutally cold frozen--

00:02:23.877 --> 00:02:25.212
- I respect that.

00:02:25.212 --> 00:02:27.147
- blowing at your face when 
you do that every day

00:02:27.147 --> 00:02:28.415
you can handle anything.

00:02:28.415 --> 00:02:29.650
- There is some truth to that.

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:31.595
Why don't you tell everybody
a little bit about your music?

00:02:31.595 --> 00:02:33.123
'Cause I think it's really cool 
and I think we'll go

00:02:33.123 --> 00:02:36.123
into the questions.

00:02:36.123 --> 00:02:38.925
No India today so
we've got a substitute.

00:02:38.925 --> 00:02:40.895
I think you're going
to do a great job.

00:02:40.895 --> 00:02:42.462
I'm very confident.
(laughter)

00:02:42.462 --> 00:02:44.163
You guys want to go a
little bit more into it?

00:02:44.163 --> 00:02:46.231
- We are electronic
dance music artists.

00:02:46.231 --> 00:02:49.114
We're singer, 
songwriters and DJs.

00:02:49.114 --> 00:02:51.104
We are currently working on

00:02:51.104 --> 00:02:54.674
our second follow-up EP to
"Ammunition" which we just

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:56.109
released a month ago.

00:02:56.109 --> 00:03:00.047
Going on the Sweat Box Tour in
fall which is in tiny venues,

00:03:00.047 --> 00:03:03.350
like sweaty punk rock venues and
we're bringing our whole rocking

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:06.119
rave style there.

00:03:06.119 --> 00:03:08.855
That's with currently
happening for us right now.

00:03:08.855 --> 00:03:12.292
- And so when I was doing a
little bit of, there's a lot of

00:03:12.292 --> 00:03:13.927
fans at Vayner of
your guys' work.

00:03:13.927 --> 00:03:16.017
There were some people are
pretty excited in New York that

00:03:16.017 --> 00:03:20.133
were sad to find out this
show was being filmed here.

00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:22.773
For the music enthusiasts
out there I kinda asked this

00:03:22.773 --> 00:03:25.072
off-camera before we started
which I hate doing which is why

00:03:25.072 --> 00:03:27.007
I'm bringing it up in what
kind of popped for you?

00:03:27.007 --> 00:03:28.508
How did you guys hit the scene?

00:03:28.508 --> 00:03:30.131
I think one of the things I'm
most fascinated by and knowing

00:03:30.131 --> 00:03:32.712
there's a lot of entrepreneurs
and business people and

00:03:32.712 --> 00:03:35.615
marketers here I do think the
internet is the true middleman.

00:03:35.615 --> 00:03:39.442
I was curious about, I asked
you, when was your pop moment?

00:03:39.442 --> 00:03:41.072
When did you guys pop and 
you kind of laughed and

00:03:41.072 --> 00:03:42.422
I laughed because

00:03:42.422 --> 00:03:44.891
everybody sees the results and
you're like it's kinda a long

00:03:44.891 --> 00:03:46.827
journey and my answer
was, "Well, of course."

00:03:46.827 --> 00:03:48.895
And I know most
people don't think that.

00:03:48.895 --> 00:03:52.084
It's always years
and years of work.

00:03:52.084 --> 00:03:52.899
What were those moments?

00:03:52.899 --> 00:03:55.151
What were the sites 
or the blogs

00:03:55.151 --> 00:03:57.871
or the moments within that

00:03:57.871 --> 00:04:01.274
ecosystem that kind of first
made it happen for you guys?

00:04:01.274 --> 00:04:03.477
- You actually mentioned 'This
Song is Sick" before we went on

00:04:03.477 --> 00:04:05.846
air and they were one of the
first blogs who posted us--

00:04:05.846 --> 00:04:06.513
- Is that right?

00:04:06.513 --> 00:04:07.848
- we freaked out
when they posted us.

00:04:07.848 --> 00:04:08.982
- Because you were
fans of it already?

00:04:08.982 --> 00:04:09.750
- Oh yeah.

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:13.386
We trolled their blog like crazy
for new music and to be posted

00:04:13.386 --> 00:04:15.188
on there was such a
huge deal for us.

00:04:15.188 --> 00:04:16.923
Then we belonged
in that community.

00:04:16.923 --> 00:04:18.190
- You love hearing that.

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:21.027
- I do because I, 
I love being transparent.

00:04:21.027 --> 00:04:22.929
A couple months ago I bought a

00:04:22.929 --> 00:04:25.098
very large stake 
in "This Song is Sick".

00:04:25.098 --> 00:04:25.766
- Amazing.

00:04:25.766 --> 00:04:28.068
- Something VaynerMedia did
for your publishing decision.

00:04:28.068 --> 00:04:30.685
I just love those guys I
just gave you some of the most

00:04:30.685 --> 00:04:34.141
authentic stuff in
indie pop mediums.

00:04:34.141 --> 00:04:36.138
Very cool. That's cool to hear.

00:04:36.776 --> 00:04:38.778
What about online video?

00:04:38.778 --> 00:04:41.882
I've seen you guys put out
several videos, they look really

00:04:41.882 --> 00:04:44.117
cool you guys should watch them.

00:04:44.117 --> 00:04:45.986
How did those go for you guys?

00:04:45.986 --> 00:04:47.721
How is that
experience in general?

00:04:47.721 --> 00:04:50.257
- I think that really
gained attention for us

00:04:50.257 --> 00:04:52.515
was the "Killin' It" video.

00:04:52.515 --> 00:04:56.930
It was the first time people saw
the face for the artist and

00:04:56.930 --> 00:05:00.934
I think that identifying was
really important for our fan and

00:05:00.934 --> 00:05:04.471
seeing what is beyond just the
song that they're listening to.

00:05:04.471 --> 00:05:08.508
And at that time there weren't
artists in the electronic dance

00:05:08.508 --> 00:05:11.478
music scene where the vocalists
were actually in the group.

00:05:11.478 --> 00:05:13.146
A lot of the time the
vocalist was a mystery.

00:05:13.146 --> 00:05:13.814
- That's right.

00:05:13.814 --> 00:05:15.856
- So you go to these festivals
and download these songs and it

00:05:15.856 --> 00:05:19.019
would be just the name of
the DJ or producer and there

00:05:19.019 --> 00:05:21.588
technically it was a ghost
singer you wouldn't know who the

00:05:21.588 --> 00:05:25.091
singer is or the vocalist or the
writer of the song is so we were

00:05:25.091 --> 00:05:28.461
one of the first groups at the
time who had a producer in the

00:05:28.461 --> 00:05:30.997
group who was Chris who is
in the group with us and then

00:05:30.997 --> 00:05:33.266
Yasmin and I being the vocalist
singers and songwriters of the

00:05:33.266 --> 00:05:33.934
group as well.

00:05:33.934 --> 00:05:35.268
- And what about
from your perspective?

00:05:35.268 --> 00:05:39.139
From your vantage point
what have you seen what's been

00:05:39.139 --> 00:05:43.410
interesting in their rise
or in the marketplace today?

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:45.579
- I just think they offer
something completely different

00:05:45.579 --> 00:05:46.880
to the space.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:50.584
There was no women whatsoever
at any of these dance music

00:05:50.584 --> 00:05:55.188
festivals and Jahan and
Yasmine had this real desire to

00:05:55.188 --> 00:05:56.389
understand the scene.

00:05:56.389 --> 00:05:59.960
They wanted to come at it from
an authentic yet very different

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:03.663
way and that worked.
And it always works online.

00:06:03.663 --> 00:06:04.914
- When it's real.
- Yeah. When it's real.

00:06:04.914 --> 00:06:06.666
- Alright let's get
into these questions.

00:06:06.666 --> 00:06:08.468
- Hey what's up
Krewella and GaryVee?

00:06:08.468 --> 00:06:10.170
My name is Steven Gold.

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:13.473
There's so many good producers
out there right now getting

00:06:13.473 --> 00:06:17.177
released on labels, getting
uploads on Sheepy and Proximity

00:06:17.177 --> 00:06:18.144
all these channels.

00:06:18.144 --> 00:06:21.948
Getting blog coverage, even
charting on Hype Machine.

00:06:21.948 --> 00:06:25.559
What separates the artist that 
get all this promotion and just

00:06:25.559 --> 00:06:28.796
get a little bit of royalties 
here and the artist that

00:06:28.796 --> 00:06:31.925
actually get to make 
a living off of music?

00:06:31.925 --> 00:06:35.762
- Anyone who isn't afraid
to experiment and I always

00:06:35.762 --> 00:06:39.132
appreciate producers when I hear
them who step outside a certain

00:06:39.132 --> 00:06:41.374
BPM or even genre.

00:06:41.374 --> 00:06:44.604
I always love risk-taking 
mentality and for me

00:06:44.604 --> 00:06:48.408
those are the people that I'll
remember for years and years and

00:06:48.408 --> 00:06:50.410
just to name a few like
Skrillex, we're big fans of

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:55.015
Skrillex, of course. Everything
that Jack Q does is really cool.

00:06:55.015 --> 00:06:59.419
Panpour Nerds we're huge
fans of them. And who else?

00:06:59.419 --> 00:07:00.854
I would say Discord love

00:07:00.854 --> 00:07:02.956
what they do as far
as experimentation.

00:07:02.956 --> 00:07:07.427
- I also think that musicians
who are able to create a song in

00:07:07.427 --> 00:07:12.232
our EDM world is amazing because
you get so used to the build up,

00:07:12.232 --> 00:07:14.601
then the drop then the break
down and the build, the drop and

00:07:14.601 --> 00:07:17.570
it just seemed so contrived
after a while but you get people

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:22.242
like Calvin Harris who make real
songs that embody so much more

00:07:22.242 --> 00:07:26.246
than just the build and the drop
and I think that is incredible.

00:07:26.246 --> 00:07:28.748
- I think my answers going to be
slightly more in the context of

00:07:28.748 --> 00:07:32.686
how you guys know that I roll
which is I think what separates

00:07:32.686 --> 00:07:35.121
is the market decides.

00:07:35.121 --> 00:07:39.359
This whole notion that there's
so much great music I think

00:07:39.359 --> 00:07:42.629
there probably is and I think
some of the great music of all

00:07:42.629 --> 00:07:46.900
time was never heard because the
market decided it wasn't great.

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:49.769
Meaning who gets to
decide what is great?

00:07:49.769 --> 00:07:52.138
And I always find
that super fascinating.

00:07:52.138 --> 00:07:54.952
It is an executive who's got
an ear like is a Clive Davis

00:07:54.952 --> 00:07:56.096
through the years?

00:07:56.096 --> 00:07:57.410
Absolutely not.

00:07:57.410 --> 00:08:01.081
It's the end market so a lot of
you email me and say I've been

00:08:01.081 --> 00:08:03.850
doing a daily vlog called
"DailyVee" and a lot of music

00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:07.487
has been given to DRock for us
and we use a lot of it and we're

00:08:07.487 --> 00:08:09.322
getting hundreds of emails now
because they are getting a lot

00:08:09.322 --> 00:08:11.658
of exposure from people that
are watching the YouTube show

00:08:11.658 --> 00:08:13.708
and it's helping them so a lot 
of people want their music on

00:08:13.708 --> 00:08:16.030
the show and everybody writes 
the same thing which is,

00:08:16.030 --> 00:08:17.597
"This is great.
My stuff is great.

00:08:17.597 --> 00:08:19.199
"Everybody tells it's great."

00:08:19.199 --> 00:08:21.368
And the answer is 
I think at some level the

00:08:21.368 --> 00:08:22.836
market gets to decide.

00:08:22.836 --> 00:08:25.038
Everybody wants to
think they're great.

00:08:25.038 --> 00:08:28.108
I always think about the way
American Idol when it first came

00:08:28.108 --> 00:08:31.678
out those people in that first
show of every season where they

00:08:31.678 --> 00:08:35.181
really truly not the people just
trying to get on TV later but

00:08:35.181 --> 00:08:37.583
those first two or three seasons
where you would just genuinely

00:08:37.583 --> 00:08:41.072
see somebody who literally
thought they were great. Right?

00:08:41.072 --> 00:08:43.388
Who literally thought they were
great and in that environment

00:08:43.388 --> 00:08:45.392
judges got to
decide if they moved on.

00:08:45.392 --> 00:08:48.194
I think what is so fascinating
about today's music marketplace

00:08:48.194 --> 00:08:51.231
and the business marketplaces
with the internet being the true

00:08:51.231 --> 00:08:54.401
middleman whether you Soundcloud
or blogs pick you up or you put

00:08:54.401 --> 00:08:59.072
out YouTube stuff or Vimeo or
whatever you do I think what

00:08:59.072 --> 00:09:01.574
separates the people 
that make a living or not

00:09:01.574 --> 00:09:03.676
is the paying customer.

00:09:03.676 --> 00:09:07.180
That enough people decide you
are great that it allows you to

00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:09.482
do it for a living.

00:09:09.482 --> 00:09:14.120
- I actually think the ones that
do it for hobby versus living

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:16.055
it's quite simply 10,000 hours.

00:09:16.055 --> 00:09:19.327
And you guys started

00:09:19.327 --> 00:09:21.227
it was very different than 
what it was four

00:09:21.227 --> 00:09:24.064
or five years later and you
guys continue to get better.

00:09:24.064 --> 00:09:26.533
- Do you think that Malcolm
Gladwell like put in the work,

00:09:26.533 --> 00:09:27.867
do you really think
that really think that?

00:09:27.867 --> 00:09:29.602
For example--
- Yes. I do.

00:09:29.602 --> 00:09:32.472
- Do you think if I put in
10,000 hours of EDM skills that

00:09:32.472 --> 00:09:34.074
I could be great at EDM.

00:09:34.074 --> 00:09:36.227
'Cause I can tell
you right now I can't.

00:09:36.227 --> 00:09:38.278
- Ok.

00:09:38.278 --> 00:09:40.713
- I genuinely think 
that talent has been

00:09:40.713 --> 00:09:42.248
stripped out of the equation.

00:09:42.248 --> 00:09:44.084
- As an artist or as a producer?

00:09:44.084 --> 00:09:46.119
- Both because I can tell 
you right now that is

00:09:46.119 --> 00:09:48.154
just not in me.

00:09:48.154 --> 00:09:50.190
- Authenticity has
to be part of it.

00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:52.091
And that's not authentic to you.

00:09:52.091 --> 00:09:53.426
- Well, that's right.
That's right.

00:09:53.426 --> 00:09:55.762
But I do think the 10,000 hour
thing is very fascinating and I

00:09:55.762 --> 00:09:57.997
do think and I talk about hustle
and hard work a lot. I just am

00:09:57.997 --> 00:10:01.434
surprised that talent is
starting to get scripted

00:10:01.434 --> 00:10:03.305
out of the equation.

00:10:03.305 --> 00:10:06.439
To be a musician like you guys
are, you guys are talented and

00:10:06.439 --> 00:10:07.373
that's a thing.

00:10:07.373 --> 00:10:09.742
- I have to interject here.
- Please.

00:10:09.742 --> 00:10:13.746
- I don't think that I, first of
all, I don't think that I'm up

00:10:13.746 --> 00:10:16.049
to par with certain
artist that I look up to.

00:10:16.049 --> 00:10:19.131
When you talk about Adele's
vocals I don't think

00:10:19.131 --> 00:10:20.285
I was born a prodigy.

00:10:20.285 --> 00:10:21.958
- But you don't need to be the
number one singer in

00:10:21.958 --> 00:10:23.079
the world to have success.

00:10:23.079 --> 00:10:25.193
- But I don't think I
was born with this--

00:10:25.193 --> 00:10:27.781
- Do you think you have a better
voice than the average hundred

00:10:27.781 --> 00:10:28.989
people out there?

00:10:28.989 --> 00:10:30.646
- No, I don't.
- Oh, yes.

00:10:30.646 --> 00:10:33.441
- The reason I say that
is because I think there's this

00:10:33.441 --> 00:10:35.719
mentality today where
people think artists on this

00:10:35.719 --> 00:10:39.005
unobtainable pedestal but if
you go back to the beginning of

00:10:39.005 --> 00:10:42.609
human civilization everyone was
sitting in a circle banging on

00:10:42.609 --> 00:10:43.943
some drums and
singing all together.

00:10:43.943 --> 00:10:46.922
It wasn't a separate
outsider, entitled group.

00:10:46.922 --> 00:10:49.148
- I think everybody can sing, 
I just don't think everyone

00:10:49.148 --> 00:10:51.851
wants to pay everybody 
to hear them to sing.

00:10:51.851 --> 00:10:53.419
- Today, I think it's different.

00:10:53.419 --> 00:10:56.022
I think it's vision, it's your
voice, it's your songwriting,

00:10:56.022 --> 00:10:59.359
it's how you curate
your music videos.

00:10:59.359 --> 00:11:00.026
It's everything.

00:11:00.026 --> 00:11:02.996
- The issue with your romantic
point of view right now is it's

00:11:02.996 --> 00:11:04.430
not being executed in reality.

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:08.368
There are hundreds of millions
of people that want to do,

00:11:08.368 --> 00:11:11.638
there's tens of millions of
Americans that want to do what

00:11:11.638 --> 00:11:13.840
you are doing right now.

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:16.910
And more interestingly and you
guys know this, you're in the

00:11:16.910 --> 00:11:19.078
scene it's much more what's
happening in entrepreneurship,

00:11:19.078 --> 00:11:22.015
it's what's
happening in athletics.

00:11:22.015 --> 00:11:25.218
There are plenty of people that
have put in lots and lots of

00:11:25.218 --> 00:11:27.854
hours especially if they
come from affluence where their

00:11:27.854 --> 00:11:30.590
parents have allowed them to
be able to go to every fucking

00:11:30.590 --> 00:11:32.859
lesson 47,000 times.

00:11:32.859 --> 00:11:34.727
Sometimes talent has to
be part of the equation.

00:11:34.727 --> 00:11:36.162
- And hunger too though.
- Sure.

00:11:36.162 --> 00:11:37.797
- Sometimes people
are given everything.

00:11:37.797 --> 00:11:39.465
- Sure. The work ethic is 
a big variable.

00:11:39.465 --> 00:11:42.168
Alright before we start getting
really testy here let's go to

00:11:42.168 --> 00:11:43.222
the second question.

00:11:43.222 --> 00:11:45.639
(silence)

00:12:17.926 --> 00:12:19.511
- Ooh, that's a good one.

00:12:19.511 --> 00:12:23.259
- I'm going to jump into this.
(crosstalk)

00:12:23.259 --> 00:12:25.894
You don't get this
question after the show?

00:12:25.894 --> 00:12:29.716
So, I think that that's a great
question by Heather I think

00:12:29.716 --> 00:12:33.386
rented land has a negative point
of view here right now in the

00:12:33.386 --> 00:12:34.721
way that she asked the question.

00:12:34.721 --> 00:12:37.724
So many people are scared that
they don't own their Facebook

00:12:37.724 --> 00:12:40.126
page, they don't own their
Snapchat account what I don't

00:12:40.126 --> 00:12:42.862
think people understand is
that's the ways it's always been

00:12:42.862 --> 00:12:44.230
about everything always.

00:12:44.230 --> 00:12:47.324
When he showed up on 
television on good day,

00:12:47.324 --> 00:12:49.444
Good Morning America or on
The Ed Sullivan show

00:12:49.444 --> 00:12:51.088
you didn't own that.

00:12:51.088 --> 00:12:55.289
You're more than welcome to be 
able to go and build your own

00:12:55.289 --> 00:12:56.709
app and things of that nature.

00:12:56.709 --> 00:13:00.093
How we got connected with
Backstage you know I'm investor

00:13:00.093 --> 00:13:04.941
in you guys where that world is 
going is very fascinating to me

00:13:04.941 --> 00:13:06.686
but I don't think it
should cripple one.

00:13:06.686 --> 00:13:10.289
Do I think that if you can own
it and execute it there and have

00:13:10.289 --> 00:13:12.659
everybody there like an email
list, like your own website,

00:13:12.659 --> 00:13:14.861
like your own app, does
that have more upside?

00:13:14.861 --> 00:13:15.862
For sure.

00:13:15.862 --> 00:13:18.197
I think there's a chicken and
egg issue though which is if

00:13:18.197 --> 00:13:22.068
nobody's there, why you go
on Snapchat and Facebook?

00:13:22.068 --> 00:13:23.703
'Cause everybody's there.

00:13:23.703 --> 00:13:27.306
And you want to siphon and I
love how people are like I'm

00:13:27.306 --> 00:13:28.941
doing all the
stuff for Facebook.

00:13:28.941 --> 00:13:31.577
No, no Facebook's doing
all the stuff for you.

00:13:31.577 --> 00:13:34.647
They've curated hundreds of
millions of people into one

00:13:34.647 --> 00:13:38.117
place that give you a chance
to be seen or heard so I think

00:13:38.117 --> 00:13:39.486
there's a little
chicken and egg thing.

00:13:39.486 --> 00:13:43.823
I do think as you gain more
traction and have more leverage

00:13:43.823 --> 00:13:46.092
that you're able to take a
little more control of your

00:13:46.092 --> 00:13:50.630
environment if you choose to but
I think both work and so to me

00:13:50.630 --> 00:13:53.132
rented space is very
comfortable to me.

00:13:53.132 --> 00:13:56.703
To me, anywhere where there's
attention is a viable place for

00:13:56.703 --> 00:14:00.940
one to speak to the world and
achieve their story outwardly

00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:04.460
and I would not be crippled
by doing either or both.

00:14:04.460 --> 00:14:05.161
- Jake.

00:14:05.161 --> 00:14:06.526
- I couldn't have
said that better.

00:14:06.526 --> 00:14:07.914
- Damn. Yeah.

00:14:07.914 --> 00:14:10.832
- I do that naturally that

00:14:10.832 --> 00:14:12.452
wasn't 10,000 hours 
of work, it was.

00:14:12.452 --> 00:14:15.916
- For me, I'm like let me let
our manager talk about that.

00:14:15.916 --> 00:14:17.945
- Alright, let's move it.

00:14:17.945 --> 00:14:20.820
- [Voiceover] Erica says, "Talk
to us about the importance of

00:14:20.820 --> 00:14:23.262
"knowing your fans and
owning your own platform!"

00:14:25.665 --> 00:14:27.333
- So much importance.

00:14:27.333 --> 00:14:31.017
I totally think you need to be
able to connect with the people

00:14:31.017 --> 00:14:34.107
who are paying for your music,
paying to come see your live

00:14:34.107 --> 00:14:38.144
shows, even not paying just
ripping offline and really

00:14:38.144 --> 00:14:39.112
loving your music.

00:14:39.112 --> 00:14:42.381
Those of the people who support
your entire career if you can't

00:14:42.381 --> 00:14:45.885
connect with them either that's
online, face-to-face at shows

00:14:45.885 --> 00:14:48.755
etc. then you have nothing.

00:14:48.755 --> 00:14:51.290
- How much time are you guys
spending and it's okay if it's a

00:14:51.290 --> 00:14:54.494
zero I'm just curious how much
time are you spending actually

00:14:54.494 --> 00:14:57.663
engaging with
your fans on social?

00:14:57.663 --> 00:14:59.098
Because for me
that feels scalable.

00:14:59.098 --> 00:15:02.679
You can't be in Des Moines, Iowa
right now but if Susie says,

00:15:02.679 --> 00:15:04.537
"I love your stuff,"
you can engage.

00:15:04.537 --> 00:15:08.174
It was such a big thing for me
in my early days but I do think

00:15:08.174 --> 00:15:10.343
that it's becoming out of fad.

00:15:10.343 --> 00:15:13.146
I think people are spending
less time today than they did 36

00:15:13.146 --> 00:15:15.047
months ago engaging with fans.

00:15:15.047 --> 00:15:16.716
I think it's a little
better decline in Twitter.

00:15:16.716 --> 00:15:19.452
I think if you look at all the
social networks besides Twitter

00:15:19.452 --> 00:15:21.254
they're more push content out.

00:15:21.254 --> 00:15:24.524
Instagram, Snapchat, YouTube you
push content out whereas Twitter

00:15:24.524 --> 00:15:27.677
when it was in it's prime was a
little more back-and-forth so

00:15:27.677 --> 00:15:29.829
I was curious, don't forget if

00:15:29.829 --> 00:15:31.697
it doesn't come natural 
to everybody.

00:15:31.697 --> 00:15:34.467
You're doing other things but
where you guys right now with

00:15:34.467 --> 00:15:37.403
literally like making a video
and being like, "Thanks, Sal,"

00:15:37.403 --> 00:15:41.841
or replying to Karen in a Snap
or engaging with a comment in

00:15:41.841 --> 00:15:43.743
Instagram and
actually replying to it?

00:15:43.743 --> 00:15:46.514
Tell the truth because I'm going
to double check and call you out

00:15:46.514 --> 00:15:50.917
if you bullshit me
here in my house.

00:15:50.917 --> 00:15:52.418
- I would say not as
much as we used to.

00:15:52.418 --> 00:15:55.935
What you're saying earlier
about how you spent 15 hours

00:15:55.935 --> 00:15:59.592
responding to comments
about an event that happened.

00:15:59.592 --> 00:16:02.662
When we first started off, Jake
was saying follow every fan.

00:16:02.662 --> 00:16:05.298
Jake, you were
really, really encouraged us.

00:16:05.298 --> 00:16:06.833
Yeah, exactly.

00:16:06.833 --> 00:16:09.168
We used to, I remember being
at the airport waiting in line

00:16:09.168 --> 00:16:09.969
responding to fans.

00:16:09.969 --> 00:16:12.738
Being in the car, responding to
fans and after certain period of

00:16:12.738 --> 00:16:13.738
time--
- You have to.

00:16:13.738 --> 00:16:17.152
I felt it actually really
worked well for us as far as

00:16:17.152 --> 00:16:19.599
building our social media fan
base but I felt like it stifled

00:16:19.599 --> 00:16:21.581
my creativity.
- Interesting.

00:16:21.581 --> 00:16:25.284
- And living, not really knowing
how to live in the real world--

00:16:25.284 --> 00:16:26.185
- Yes.

00:16:26.185 --> 00:16:29.222
- in a way I felt every moment
I had to wait or every moment

00:16:29.222 --> 00:16:30.523
I didn't have to 
talk to someone.

00:16:30.523 --> 00:16:33.526
Every moment I was sitting
at a table I was on my phone.

00:16:33.526 --> 00:16:36.462
Nowadays, I actually practiced
just giving myself an allotment

00:16:36.462 --> 00:16:39.866
of time and I feel like our fan
base is really understanding of

00:16:39.866 --> 00:16:41.495
that because we've been
pretty vocal of that.

00:16:41.495 --> 00:16:43.002
- Interesting.

00:16:43.002 --> 00:16:45.004
- I don't think they think they
take it personally that it takes

00:16:45.004 --> 00:16:45.838
us five days to respond.

00:16:45.838 --> 00:16:47.607
- That's your authentic place.
- Yeah.

00:16:47.607 --> 00:16:50.343
I also feel like spiritually I'm
going one direction where I want

00:16:50.343 --> 00:16:52.078
to spend less time online,--
- Yes.

00:16:52.078 --> 00:16:53.379
- business-wise I understand

00:16:53.379 --> 00:16:54.881
that it's so 
important to engage.

00:16:54.881 --> 00:16:55.548
- Of course.

00:16:55.548 --> 00:16:57.216
- That's why we do
have people like Jake.

00:16:57.216 --> 00:16:58.584
- What about you?

00:16:58.584 --> 00:16:59.819
- I go through phases.

00:16:59.819 --> 00:17:01.487
We just released
an EP a month ago.

00:17:01.487 --> 00:17:06.157
I was online the entire week
pretty much just responding to

00:17:06.157 --> 00:17:07.059
people consistently.

00:17:07.059 --> 00:17:11.364
Kinda went downhill after there
and I probably spend a good

00:17:11.364 --> 00:17:15.166
three hours every week just
responding to people every

00:17:15.166 --> 00:17:17.731
Monday or every Friday I
just sit down and respond.

00:17:17.731 --> 00:17:20.006
- And where?
What platforms?

00:17:20.006 --> 00:17:21.707
- Twitter mainly.
That's the only one.

00:17:21.707 --> 00:17:24.109
- Are you guys producing 
content for Snapchat?

00:17:24.109 --> 00:17:26.178
- No.
- You really, really need to.

00:17:26.178 --> 00:17:27.666
(laughter)

00:17:27.666 --> 00:17:30.616
- I just want to say as an
artist it is really important to

00:17:30.616 --> 00:17:33.096
have a marketing firm, have
management, have friends,

00:17:33.096 --> 00:17:36.055
have people to 
help curate content.

00:17:36.055 --> 00:17:39.325
What we do is we sit down, we
have a meeting once a week, hey,

00:17:39.325 --> 00:17:41.327
look at all these pictures.
These are fun pictures we've

00:17:41.327 --> 00:17:43.876
taken, this is what we did,
here's what we want to say about

00:17:43.876 --> 00:17:46.599
it and have someone else kind 
of do that work on that

00:17:46.599 --> 00:17:47.300
and so it's not.

00:17:47.300 --> 00:17:49.835
- I think I don't think that's
an artist statement, I think

00:17:49.835 --> 00:17:51.974
it's great that you have
self-awareness to know what

00:17:51.974 --> 00:17:53.205
works for you.

00:17:53.205 --> 00:17:55.308
'Cause I think a certain artists
they should be doing a ton of

00:17:55.308 --> 00:17:57.209
that because it what
comes natural to them.

00:17:57.209 --> 00:17:59.879
A lot of my business friend
contemporaries are like, "You're

00:17:59.879 --> 00:18:01.818
running all these businesses. 
Why are you spending

00:18:01.818 --> 00:18:03.349
four hours a day 
engaging with fans?"

00:18:03.349 --> 00:18:04.850
I'm like, "That's
my natural state.

00:18:04.850 --> 00:18:07.420
"It's where I get my information
from. It's when I want to do."

00:18:07.420 --> 00:18:09.655
But I don't think that's
what everybody should do.

00:18:09.655 --> 00:18:12.091
I like listening to the 
way you guys answered.

00:18:12.091 --> 00:18:16.262
What I like is I just think you
guys are figuring yourselves out

00:18:16.262 --> 00:18:19.031
and putting yourself in the best
position to succeed and I think

00:18:19.031 --> 00:18:21.267
really that
ultimately is the main play.

00:18:21.267 --> 00:18:21.934
I really do.

00:18:21.934 --> 00:18:27.640
- I think fans crave an
experience, a story much more

00:18:27.640 --> 00:18:31.177
and content much more than
they crave whether or not you're

00:18:31.177 --> 00:18:33.012
responding to them.
- I disagree.

00:18:33.012 --> 00:18:37.817
I would actually argue and you
can be right but I'm completely

00:18:37.817 --> 00:18:41.087
and I have a lot of data to
support this believe that access

00:18:41.087 --> 00:18:43.356
is the most valuable thing 
an artist can now

00:18:43.356 --> 00:18:44.490
bring to the table.
- I agree.

00:18:44.490 --> 00:18:46.726
- Access meaning
that you're accessible?

00:18:46.726 --> 00:18:49.929
- Like some sort of access.
- Like happy birthday to you.

00:18:49.929 --> 00:18:51.330
- I think you can
touch a movement.

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:54.400
You do it with a lot of brands
if a brand doesn't respond to

00:18:54.400 --> 00:18:57.369
questions within four hours--
- Sure.

00:18:57.369 --> 00:18:59.153
- that's a problem. 
But when you're an artist your

00:18:59.153 --> 00:19:01.707
responsibility is 
to create amazing art.

00:19:01.707 --> 00:19:02.742
- I think that's for sure.

00:19:02.742 --> 00:19:06.579
First of all, no good
marketing solves a shit product.

00:19:06.579 --> 00:19:10.616
If you guys engage 24/7 with
everybody but your music sucked

00:19:10.616 --> 00:19:12.018
shit, you would lose.

00:19:12.018 --> 00:19:13.419
On the flip side,

00:19:13.419 --> 00:19:16.922
I do think that people
really underestimate.

00:19:16.922 --> 00:19:20.993
I can promise you right now your
top 5,000 fans would shit their

00:19:20.993 --> 00:19:23.793
pants if you reply
to them on Snapchat.

00:19:23.793 --> 00:19:25.568
- I don't even
know how to do that.

00:19:25.568 --> 00:19:28.028
(crosstalk and laughter)

00:19:28.028 --> 00:19:29.614
- I don't even
know, how do you reply?

00:19:29.614 --> 00:19:32.004
- We're not going to do this
right now because we're still in

00:19:32.004 --> 00:19:33.506
the middle of the show.

00:19:33.506 --> 00:19:36.842
What I'm going to do right
now is even more interesting.

00:19:36.842 --> 00:19:40.946
Guys this is my snap, can you
guys make a commitment to get

00:19:40.946 --> 00:19:41.947
serious about Snap?

00:19:41.947 --> 00:19:43.015
- Oh God, no.
- Please.

00:19:43.015 --> 00:19:46.018
- I'm sorry, I can't.
- Please.

00:19:46.018 --> 00:19:47.469
- I'll try. I'll try.

00:19:47.469 --> 00:19:52.458
See the thing is about Snapchat
the reason it's the one platform

00:19:52.458 --> 00:19:54.860
that I do not use
because it's the one,--

00:19:54.860 --> 00:19:57.396
- See you're not even listening.
- I don't understand.

00:19:57.396 --> 00:19:58.357
- I'm listening to you.

00:19:58.357 --> 00:19:59.737
- How do you do that and
listen at the same time?

00:19:59.737 --> 00:20:01.152
- Easy.
- Your generation, man.

00:20:01.152 --> 00:20:02.340
- No, no, no.

00:20:02.340 --> 00:20:07.506
I think that it's behavior.
Right? It's the 10,000 hours.

00:20:07.506 --> 00:20:09.008
You put in the
work, you can do it.

00:20:09.008 --> 00:20:11.277
- You can multitask like
that for 10,000 hours.

00:20:11.277 --> 00:20:13.412
- I think you
multi-task quite a bit.

00:20:13.412 --> 00:20:14.447
- I do.

00:20:14.447 --> 00:20:15.809
- Of course, it's
just what we do.

00:20:15.809 --> 00:20:17.567
Let's move on.

00:20:17.567 --> 00:20:19.556
- [Voiceover] Tom asks, 
"How'd you girls get hooked

00:20:19.556 --> 00:20:21.537
"up with Jake Udell?"

00:20:21.537 --> 00:20:24.638
How big of an influence
has it had on your career?"

00:20:24.638 --> 00:20:26.609
- High school.
- Yep.

00:20:26.609 --> 00:20:28.120
I graduated with Jake Udell.

00:20:28.120 --> 00:20:29.584
Jake was in my science, 
what was it?

00:20:29.584 --> 00:20:31.574
Which science class?
Was it biology?

00:20:31.574 --> 00:20:33.254
All I know is I got a D.

00:20:33.254 --> 00:20:34.338
Got a D.

00:20:34.338 --> 00:20:36.936
(laughter)

00:20:36.936 --> 00:20:39.338
And Jake you were
actually if you want to talk

00:20:39.338 --> 00:20:40.206
about your music career.

00:20:40.206 --> 00:20:42.074
You were pursuing
being an artist.

00:20:42.074 --> 00:20:44.996
- I was an awful rapper.
Like the worst.

00:20:44.996 --> 00:20:46.812
DJ Khaled and DJ Drama
actually posted my mix tape.

00:20:46.812 --> 00:20:49.281
- Why didn't you just
put in the 10,000 hours?

00:20:49.281 --> 00:20:50.783
- I did. I did.
- And become--

00:20:50.783 --> 00:20:52.751
- So here's the thing 
I gave up on my

00:20:52.751 --> 00:20:54.487
10,000 hours as a musician--

00:20:54.487 --> 00:20:56.589
- Because you
didn't have the talent.

00:20:57.819 --> 00:21:00.772
- Okay, I'll admit that.
- Jake has this swag.

00:21:00.772 --> 00:21:03.728
- See here's the thing, I made 
a pivot and said okay--

00:21:03.728 --> 00:21:05.039
- Because you were smart.

00:21:05.039 --> 00:21:07.311
Because not everybody 
can do anything

00:21:07.311 --> 00:21:09.162
if they put in 10,000 hours.

00:21:09.162 --> 00:21:10.636
- I actually believe, I believe
that if you put in the 10,000

00:21:10.636 --> 00:21:11.737
hours it can happen.

00:21:11.737 --> 00:21:14.306
I'm not saying you can be
performing at the Grammys but

00:21:14.306 --> 00:21:16.775
you can it's possible
to have a hit record.

00:21:16.775 --> 00:21:18.241
I believe that can happen.

00:21:18.241 --> 00:21:21.180
- Okay. Anything can happen.

00:21:21.180 --> 00:21:23.649
But it doesn't
consistently happen.

00:21:23.649 --> 00:21:25.284
To me that's the
point which is like--

00:21:25.284 --> 00:21:27.553
- That's what's so fascinating
about what Malcolm said though.

00:21:27.553 --> 00:21:30.289
Malcolm said he couldn't find
people that have put in the

00:21:30.289 --> 00:21:31.924
10,000 hours that
hadn't made it.

00:21:31.924 --> 00:21:33.859
Of course 'cause their stores
weren't known because he was

00:21:33.859 --> 00:21:36.962
trying to find them and
he couldn't find them.

00:21:36.962 --> 00:21:40.533
- How many hours did
you put into rapping?

00:21:40.533 --> 00:21:42.201
- Oh my gosh.
- Exactly.

00:21:42.201 --> 00:21:44.203
- Not 10,000 though, 
not even close.

00:21:44.203 --> 00:21:45.404
- But that's impossible.

00:21:45.404 --> 00:21:48.340
If you suck shit at something
and you put 10,000 hours you're

00:21:48.340 --> 00:21:50.276
not going to become
one of the greats in it.

00:21:50.276 --> 00:21:52.745
- Right.
- I was a better marketer.

00:21:52.745 --> 00:21:55.814
- There's enormous amounts of
kids, every single kid that

00:21:55.814 --> 00:21:58.017
tried become a professional
athlete that didn't become a

00:21:58.017 --> 00:22:01.020
professional athlete which is
almost everybody put in all the

00:22:01.020 --> 00:22:04.567
hours from first grade to
senior year and didn't make it.

00:22:04.567 --> 00:22:07.760
- 10,000 though? That's the 
thing when you look at that--

00:22:07.760 --> 00:22:10.329
- I don't know the math
on what 10,000 hours is.

00:22:10.329 --> 00:22:13.499
- Did I spent 10,000 hours for
my rap career or was I 10,000

00:22:13.499 --> 00:22:14.200
hours in the studio?

00:22:14.200 --> 00:22:17.102
I was definitely not 10,000
hours in the studio trying to be

00:22:17.102 --> 00:22:19.471
the best rapper.
- I love Malcolm.

00:22:19.471 --> 00:22:21.229
Nobody can convince me.

00:22:21.229 --> 00:22:24.710
If that was true then we should
tell every six-year-old right

00:22:24.710 --> 00:22:27.613
now to spend every minute of
your time on the number one

00:22:27.613 --> 00:22:29.648
thing that you want to be and
you will become that and

00:22:29.648 --> 00:22:31.250
that is absolute bullshit.

00:22:31.250 --> 00:22:33.852
- I think that's
absolutely true.

00:22:33.852 --> 00:22:35.988
